<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bastar State</h1><p class="st-meta">1324 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Jagdalpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A vast forest kingdom in central India founded by refugees from the Kakatiya court, famous for its goddess Danteshwari and its 75-day Dussehra festival.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annama-deva">Annama Deva</a>, a brother of the last Kakatiya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-kakatiya">Prataparudra</a>, fled north after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> in 1323 and established a kingdom among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond</a> and other peoples of the Bastar plateau. The family goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danteshwari">Danteshwari</a>, enshrined at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantewada">Dantewada</a>, became the patron deity of the state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bastar was thinly populated, densely forested and overwhelmingly Adivasi, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond</a>, Maria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muria">Muria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halba">Halba</a> communities. The Rajas ruled through local headmen and ritual ties, celebrated in the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bastar-dussehra">Bastar Dussehra</a>. In the 18th century the state became tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagpur-kingdom">Nagpur Kingdom</a>, passing under British control in 1853. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumkal-rebellion">Bhumkal rebellion</a> of 1910 against forest restrictions was suppressed by British troops.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bastar merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, in 1948. Its former ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravir-chandra-bhanj-deo">Pravir Chandra Bhanj Deo</a> was killed by police at his palace in 1966. Bastar is now a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bastar-state.jpg" alt="Bastar State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: GourgPie · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bastar_State.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1324 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jagdalpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Annama Deva, Pravir Chandra Bhanj Deo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Adivasi religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Halbi, Gondi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bastar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=bastar-state" aria-label="Bastar State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6899 1471 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bastar State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7219 1727L7126 1652M7219 1727L7364 1602M7219 1727L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7126 1652h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7219 1727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7219 1727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jagdalpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=bastar-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
